These materials were first dry pre-mixed, than wet mixed, dried and fired at 1100 °C. 10 Parts of this coloured-frit together with 90 parts of a transparent basic ceramic frit and 8 parts of kaolin formed a red coloured stable glaze.

From the end of the 70ties these inclusion pigments found their way into ceramic application. The fact, however, that the colour saturation was rather low meant that these stains of the first generation of inclusion pigments could not be applied e.g. as strong decoration red colour or as glaze stain in case that a real bright red colour was required with a wavelength of about 614 nm and high colour saturation. One of the typical applications was therefore in the sanitary industry. Even with low additions of this stain they gave good stability to make trend colours as Bahama beige, Carneol, Caramel, Sunset, Stella, Magnolia and Anemone.#footnote[Klaus P. Gempper. 'The growing role of inclusion pigments stains in the ceramic industry'. Degussa AG, Cer. Col. Division.]

V. Lambies Lavilla and J. M. Rincón López#footnote[V. Lambies Lavilla; J. M. Rincón López. 'Study of the Mechanism of Formation of a Zircon-Cadmium Sulphoselenide Pigment'. Trans. Journal British Cer. Society, 80, 1981.] gave an explanation with regard to the forming of the #ch("ZrSiO4 * Cd * S * Se") inclusion pigments, mentioning the reactions which take place for the forming of the pigment. They studied mixtures with #ch("ZrO2"), #ch("SiO2"), #ch("CdS"), #ch("CdCO3"), #ch("S"), #ch("Se") and the mineralisers #ch("NaF") and #ch("LiF"). See @tab:compositions-inclusion-pigments for the mixtures. The mixtures were fired at 900 °C in an electrical furnace with a heating rate of 300 °C p/h and 1/2h soaking at top temperature.

Following the article, the authors concluded after investigations with XR-D and SEM analyses that the formation of small crystals of #ch("ZrSiO4") takes place between #ch("ZrO2"), #ch("SiO2") and the mineralizer according to the reactions as proposed by Eppler. Simultaneously hexagonal crystals of #ch("Cd(S_x Se_(1-x))") are formed. In the main stage the crystals of #ch("Cd(S_x Se_(1-x))") are trapped by zircon. This phenomenon can be explained in terms of the theory of sintering in the presence of a liquid phase.

