After the invention of the inclusion pigments and creating the 1st generation of these type of inclusion #ch("CdSSe") pigments into a zircon silicate crystal, the developments didn't stop and based upon new technologies stronger pigments have been created also by the company Broll Buntpigmente, e.g. red pigment 3033 and others.

Also in the early 80ties some attempts have been made to apply another method to protect the #ch("CdSSe") stains from an early contact with molten glaze components. This was done with a CVD process (Chemical Vapour Deposition) in cooperation with the Technical University in Eindhoven.#footnote[Corrrespondence between author and Ir. Adriaansen, TU Eindhoven, 1987.] In this process a reactor is used where powder material (like a #ch("CdSSe") stain) can be brought in a vacuum and at higher temperatures and pressure. Various gasses can be led into the reactor to create a coating around the (powder) material. The tests were done to coat the material among other things with the transparent material #ch("Si3N4"). These first attempts did not give enough positive results for continuation.

Because of the threat of a complete ban on cadmium during the 80ties one was looking for different alternatives. The yellow #ch("CdS") pigment, in the hexagonal crystal lattice, has many proven properties over (organic based) yellow colours. Only a low amount of the pigment is necessary to give a strong yellow colour in plastics (0.4 -- 0.6 %). Also the high temperature resistance during processing of the plastics is a great advantage. The company Colorsil BV in The Netherlands (a joint-venture of Reimbold & Strick and Mr. Ch. Zwart) made attempts in the late 80ties to replace the #ch("CdS") yellow by zircon silicate 'doped' yellow pigments, viz. zircon-praeseodimium yellow (#ch("ZrSiPr")) for application in plastics. One of the problems was that the zircon silicate material made the plastic more stiff and had also other negative impacts on the chemical/physical properties. Also the average particle size was too high and had to go down to a $D^50$ of 4 #ch("mu")m. In cooperation with the Technical University of Delft they tried to find ways to connect organic 'tails' to the zircon silicate material to make it fit better in the plastic materials.#footnote[Dr. Ir. K. te Nijenhuis. Interview in Pt-Aktueel, nr.46, 1989; Chemisch Weekblad, nr.47, 1989.] This method was successful and starting from 1994 no cadmium pigments were used anymore e.g. for the famous beer crates of some of the biggest breweries worldwide. Instead, one applied specially prepared and coated zircon-praeseodimium yellow stains for the yellow colour. This continued till about 2001 when other organic green and yellow phthalo pigments came on the market, replacing the zircon-praeseodimium stains for economical reasons.

Besides the inclusion pigments as manufactured by the company Broll Buntpigmente, which soon will likely be the only manufacturer for these type of pigments left in Europe, another and third generation of very strong cadmium stains came on the market after 2000. This generation was developed and made in China and showed a stronger colour compared to the existing and available stains from West European origin. The internal Chinese market is demanding huge amounts of products and also red stains for the ceramic industry which is affecting prices of cadmium and selenium remarkably ever since about 2002. The Chinese manufacturers have found a way to produce the encapsulated #ch("CdSSe") pigments resulting in much stronger red, orange and yellow pigments than the existing ones.#footnote['Preparation of #ch("ZrSiO4 * Cd(S_x Se_(1-x))") pigments', CeramicStudies Journal, 1998-02.]#footnote['Synthetic Mechanism of Zircon Based Ceramic Pigments, Journal of Shandong Instituteof Building Materials, 1998-01.]#footnote['Study on the Preparation of Cadmium Red Pigment by Indirect Sedimentation - Calcination Method', Inorganic Chemicals Industry, 2000-03.]#footnote['Research and Development on Red Ceramic Pigment', Jiangxi Chemical Industry, 2001-04.]#footnote['Development of a New Type Red Glaze for the Decoration of Domestic Porcelain and Artistic Porcelain', China Ceramic Industry, 2002-05.] On top of that the production volumes are that high that there is also volume available for the export market. An estimation of the actual Chinese production of cadmium stains now (2010) is that the production of the cadmium stains is amounting to about 800 MT / month. An estimated production e.g. at Wanxing company is approximately 60 MT / month; at Jino approximately 80 MT/month and there are at least more than 10 other factories more producing cadmium pigments like Baifu, Hangzhou Hongyan Pigment Chemical Ltd Comp. (hy-pigment) and others. These factories will undoubtedly come to other than the Chinese markets with attractive prices for their strong cadmium selenium encapsulated stains. A company as Wangxing e.g. has already facilities in Turkey, Italy, Spain and Mexico.

