With these strong stains one can obtain strong and stable red coloured glazes also at rather high firing temperatures up to 1200 °C. The vase as shown on @photo:transparent-glaze-cdse is an example of a cadmium selenium red glaze made with one of the strong red stains. The glaze is a lead free transparent one with reference ECR10493-P and the glaze is coloured with 10 % of the strong red stain 14P8003.

These very strong red stains have also been applied to develop strong inglaze red decoration colours e.g. for firing at a level of 1180 °C till 1220 °C in about 90 minutes. The strong pigments are used in combinations with suitable flux systems to develop new ranges of red, orange and yellow inglaze colours which now show a colourstrength and colourstability which were not possible some years ago.

It is known that China intends to be in line with international health and safety regulations, despite the actual state of some cadmium selenium stain production sites now and in the past. It is already announced in China that new regulations with regard to health and safety of different materials, also cadmium, will apply in China as from 2012. This will probably affect the production of these stains in the near future.

Now, 2010, the ceramic industry is able to produce very strong red, orange and yellow #ch("CdSSe") stains which enable manufacturers to produce really laquer-like ceramic products, no matter if it is for wall- or floortiles, vases, giftware or dinnerware and at any temperature which is required by the kiln conditions. An achievement which could be dreamed of only some decades back.
